          "id": "prototyping-model",
          "title": "Prototyping Model",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 1 Concept 19",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Prototyping Model: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Prototyping Model\n\nThe Prototyping Model creates an early working model of the system to understand and validate requirements.\n\nDesign:\n\n```text\nInitial Requirements\n              │\n              ▼\n       Quick Design\n              │\n              ▼\n        Build Prototype\n              │\n              ▼\n       Customer Evaluation\n              │\n        ┌─────┴─────┐\n        ▼           ▼\n     Accept      Changes\n        │           │\n        │           ▼\n        │      Refine Prototype\n        │           │\n        └─────◄─────┘\n              │\n              ▼\n       Final Development\n```\n\nUseful when requirements are unclear or customers need to see an early version.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nThe **Prototyping Model** is a software development life cycle (SDLC) model in which a **throwaway or evolutionary working model** of the system — called a *prototype* — is constructed early in the project to **understand and validate requirements** before full-scale development begins. It is classified as an **evolutionary / iterative process model** because the prototype is repeatedly refined based on **customer evaluation** until the customer **accepts** it, after which **final development** of the production system proceeds.\n\nFormally, the Prototyping Model can be viewed as a feedback-driven process where the requirement specification is not frozen upfront but is *discovered* through successive cycles of **Quick Design → Build Prototype → Customer Evaluation → Refine Prototype**.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe model operates on the principle that **users often cannot fully articulate requirements** until they see a tangible version of the system. The workflow is:\n\n1. **Initial Requirements** — A preliminary, often incomplete, set of requirements is gathered.\n2. **Quick Design** — A fast, high-level design focusing on aspects visible to the customer (UI, inputs, outputs) is prepared.\n3. **Build Prototype** — A working model is constructed quickly, not necessarily with production-quality code.\n4. **Customer Evaluation** — The customer uses the prototype and provides feedback.\n5. **Decision Point** — Two outcomes are possible:\n   - **Accept** → proceed to **Final Development**.\n   - **Changes** → **Refine Prototype**, then loop back to **Customer Evaluation**.\n6. **Final Development** — Once accepted, the real system is engineered with proper quality standards.\n\nThe loop between **Customer Evaluation** and **Refine Prototype** continues until the customer accepts the prototype.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n- **Unclear requirements scenario:** A customer requests a \"reporting dashboard\" without specifying metrics. A prototype with sample charts is built; the customer then identifies the exact metrics needed.\n- **Early-version validation:** When a customer needs to *see an early version* before committing, the prototype serves as a communication artifact.\n- **Iterative refinement example:** A prototype is shown → customer requests changes → prototype is refined → shown again → accepted → final development begins.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet the prototype refinement process be modeled as a sequence of iterations:\n\n$$\nP_0 \\xrightarrow{E} F_1 \\xrightarrow{R} P_1 \\xrightarrow{E} F_2 \\xrightarrow{R} P_2 \\cdots \\xrightarrow{E} \\text{Accept}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- \\(P_i\\) = prototype at iteration \\(i\\)\n- \\(E\\) = Customer Evaluation function\n- \\(R\\) = Refine Prototype function\n- \\(F_i\\) = feedback at iteration \\(i\\)\n\nThe process terminates when:\n\n$$\nE(P_n) = \\text{Accept}\n$$\n\nTotal effort can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Effort} = \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\left( C_{\\text{design}} + C_{\\text{build}}(P_i) + C_{\\text{eval}}(P_i) + C_{\\text{refine}}(P_i) \\right) + C_{\\text{final}}\n$$\n\nWhere \\(n\\) is the number of refinement cycles before acceptance.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Prototyping Model | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Requirement clarity | Used when requirements are **unclear** | Core motivation |\n| Customer involvement | High — **customer evaluation** is a formal step | Feedback loop is central |\n| Early version visibility | Yes — customer **needs to see an early version** | Key benefit |\n| Iteration | Yes — **Refine Prototype** loop | Until **Accept** |\n| Transition to production | Via **Final Development** | After acceptance |\n| Design style | **Quick Design** | Fast, not exhaustive |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- Skipping **Customer Evaluation** and moving directly to **Final Development**.\n- Treating the prototype as the final product without performing **Final Development**.\n- Spending too long on **Quick Design** instead of keeping it quick.\n- Ignoring the **Changes → Refine Prototype** loop when the customer does not **Accept**.\n- Assuming requirements are clear when the situation actually calls for a prototype.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the flow: **Initial Requirements → Quick Design → Build Prototype → Customer Evaluation → (Accept | Changes) → Final Development**.\n- Emphasize the **two branches** at Customer Evaluation: **Accept** and **Changes**.\n- State the key use case: **requirements are unclear** or **customers need to see an early version**.\n- Mention the **Refine Prototype** loop explicitly — it is the defining iterative feature.\n- Draw the diagram accurately; marks are often awarded for correct flow and branch labels.",
