          "id": "design-model",
          "title": "Design Model",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 2 Concept 4",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Design Model: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Design Model\n\nA design model represents different aspects of the software system before implementation.\n\nIt may describe:\n\n- Data\n- Architecture\n- Components\n- Interfaces\n- Interactions\n- Processing\n\nDesign Model:\n\n```text\nDESIGN MODEL\n                         │\n       ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐\n       ▼                 ▼                 ▼\n  Architectural       Data Model       Interface\n     Model                                Model\n       │                 │                 │\n       ▼                 ▼                 ▼\n Components          Data Objects       User/System\n Relationships       Structures         Interfaces\n       │\n       ▼\n Component Design\n       │\n       ▼\n Detailed Design\n```\n\nThe design model provides a representation that developers can use to construct the actual software.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nThe **Design Model** is a structured representation of the software system that is created during the **design phase** of the software development life cycle, prior to implementation. It captures the different aspects of the system — including **data**, **architecture**, **components**, **interfaces**, **interactions**, and **processing** — so that developers have a complete blueprint for constructing the actual software.\n\nIn formal terms, the design model is the bridge between the **analysis model** (what the system must do) and the **implementation** (how the system is built). It translates requirements into a form that can be directly realized in code.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe design model operates on the principle of **separation of concerns through multiple views**. Rather than describing the system in a single monolithic representation, it decomposes the system into complementary sub-models, each addressing a distinct aspect:\n\n1. **Architectural Model** — captures the overall structure of the system, including components and their relationships.\n2. **Data Model** — captures data objects and their structures.\n3. **Interface Model** — captures user and system interfaces.\n4. **Component Design** — refines architectural components into implementable units.\n5. **Detailed Design** — specifies the internal logic and processing of each component.\n\nEach sub-model refines the previous level, moving from high-level structure toward implementation-ready detail. The design model thus provides a representation that developers can use to construct the actual software.\n\n### Hierarchical Decomposition\n\n```text\nDESIGN MODEL\n     │\n     ├── Architectural Model ──► Components, Relationships\n     │                                │\n     │                                ▼\n     │                          Component Design\n     │                                │\n     │                                ▼\n     │                          Detailed Design\n     │\n     ├── Data Model ──► Data Objects, Structures\n     │\n     └── Interface Model ──► User/System Interfaces\n```\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Architectural Model\nFor a banking application, the architectural model might show components such as *Account Manager*, *Transaction Processor*, and *Authentication Service*, along with the relationships (e.g., Transaction Processor depends on Account Manager).\n\n### Example 2: Data Model\nThe same system's data model would describe **data objects** such as `Account`, `Customer`, and `Transaction`, and their **structures** (fields, types, and associations).\n\n### Example 3: Interface Model\nThe interface model would specify **user interfaces** (e.g., login screen, transfer form) and **system interfaces** (e.g., APIs to external payment gateways).\n\n### Example 4: Component and Detailed Design\nThe architectural component *Transaction Processor* is refined during **component design** into modules, and further refined during **detailed design** into algorithms and processing logic.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe design model can be viewed as a set of interrelated sub-models:\n\n$$\n\\text{Design Model} = \\{ A, D, I, C, P \\}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\( A \\) = Architectural Model\n- \\( D \\) = Data Model\n- \\( I \\) = Interface Model\n- \\( C \\) = Component Design\n- \\( P \\) = Detailed Design (Processing)\n\nThe refinement relationship between levels can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Architectural Model} \\rightarrow \\text{Component Design} \\rightarrow \\text{Detailed Design}\n$$\n\nEach arrow denotes increasing level of detail, moving from abstract structure to concrete implementation guidance.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Architectural Model | Data Model | Interface Model | Component/Detailed Design |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Focus | Components & relationships | Data objects & structures | User/System interfaces | Internal logic & processing |\n| Level | High-level | Structural | Boundary-level | Low-level |\n| Output | System structure | Data structures | Interface specifications | Implementable modules |\n| Refines | Requirements | Data requirements | Interaction requirements | Architectural components |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing the design model with the analysis model** — the design model describes *how* the system is structured, not just *what* it must do.\n- **Omitting one of the sub-models** — all aspects (data, architecture, components, interfaces, interactions, processing) must be represented.\n- **Skipping refinement levels** — jumping directly from the architectural model to code without component design and detailed design.\n- **Treating the design model as implementation** — it is a representation used *before* implementation, not the implementation itself.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the **six aspects** the design model may describe: **Data, Architecture, Components, Interfaces, Interactions, Processing**.\n- Be able to reproduce the **Design Model diagram** showing Architectural Model, Data Model, and Interface Model branching from the Design Model.\n- Emphasize that the design model provides a representation that **developers can use to construct the actual software**.\n- Link the refinement chain: **Architectural Model → Component Design → Detailed Design**.\n- Use the comparative table to quickly contrast the sub-models in structured questions.",
