          "id": "oo-analysis",
          "title": "OO Analysis",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 2 Concept 19",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on OO Analysis: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## OO Analysis\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Object-Oriented Analysis (OOA)** is the phase of the object-oriented software development lifecycle in which the problem domain is examined in terms of **objects, classes, responsibilities, and relationships**. It is a conceptual modelling activity: the analyst studies *what* the system must do and *which real-world entities* it must represent, deliberately postponing decisions about *how* the system will be implemented.\n\nOOA produces a **model of the problem domain** — not a model of the solution. The entities discovered during OOA (such as `Student`, `Teacher`, `Course`, and `Department`) are abstractions of things that exist in the real world being modelled, and they are identified **before implementation details are finalized**.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe working principle of OOA can be summarized as follows:\n\n1. **Domain investigation** — The analyst studies the problem domain (e.g., a college) to understand the entities, processes, and rules that operate within it.\n2. **Object identification** — Real-world things that have identity, state, and behaviour are identified as candidate objects. In the college example, these are `Student`, `Teacher`, `Course`, and `Department`.\n3. **Class formation** — Objects with common structure and behaviour are grouped into classes (the class `Student` describes all individual students).\n4. **Responsibility assignment** — Each class is given the responsibilities (knowledge and actions) it must hold in the model.\n5. **Relationship modelling** — Associations and structural links between classes are captured. The college diagram shows `Student`, `Teacher`, and `Course` all connected to `Department`.\n6. **Deferral of implementation** — Only after the domain model is stable are implementation details (language, data structures, algorithms) considered.\n\nThe key idea is that the analysis identifies **objects existing in the problem domain** first; implementation concerns come later.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n#### Example: College System\n\nThe existing model is reproduced below:\n\n```text\nCOLLEGE SYSTEM\n                    │\n       ┌────────────┼────────────┐\n       ▼            ▼            ▼\n    Student       Teacher       Course\n       │            │            │\n       │            │            │\n       └────────────┼────────────┘\n                    ▼\n               Department\n```\n\n**Interpretation of the model:**\n\n| Element | Role in the Problem Domain |\n|---|---|\n| `Student` | An object/class representing a person enrolled in the college |\n| `Teacher` | An object/class representing a person who teaches |\n| `Course` | An object/class representing a unit of study offered by the college |\n| `Department` | An object/class that groups and connects `Student`, `Teacher`, and `Course` |\n\nThe diagram shows three classes (`Student`, `Teacher`, `Course`) linked to a common class (`Department`). This reflects the domain fact that students, teachers, and courses all belong to a department within the college system.\n\n**What OOA does *not* do here:** it does not decide how `Student` will be stored in a database, which programming language will implement `Course`, or what algorithms compute a grade. Those are implementation decisions that come after analysis.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nOOA is primarily a modelling activity, but its structural results can be described formally.\n\nLet the problem domain be a set of entities:\n\n$$\nD = \\{e_1, e_2, \\dots, e_n\\}\n$$\n\nA **class** is an equivalence grouping of entities that share common structure and behaviour:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ e \\in D \\mid \\text{properties}(e) \\text{ are shared} \\}\n$$\n\nFor the college example:\n\n$$\n\\text{Classes} = \\{\\, Student,\\ Teacher,\\ Course,\\ Department \\,\\}\n$$\n\nA **relationship** between two classes can be expressed as a relation:\n\n$$\nR \\subseteq C_i \\times C_j\n$$\n\nThe college diagram expresses relationships of the form:\n\n$$\nR(Student, Department),\\quad R(Teacher, Department),\\quad R(Course, Department)\n$$\n\nThese relationships are identified during analysis, **before implementation details are finalized**.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | OO Analysis (OOA) | Implementation (later phase) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Focus | Problem domain | Solution domain |\n| Question answered | *What* exists and what must be done | *How* it will be built |\n| Output | Objects, classes, responsibilities, relationships | Code, data structures, algorithms |\n| Example artifact | College System class diagram | Program implementing `Student`, `Teacher`, `Course`, `Department` |\n| Timing | Before implementation details are finalized | After analysis is complete |\n| Level of abstraction | Conceptual / real-world entities | Concrete / executable constructs |\n\nThe essential contrast: OOA identifies **objects existing in the problem domain**, while implementation realizes them in software.\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Jumping to implementation too early.** OOA must identify domain objects *before implementation details are finalized*; choosing data structures or languages during analysis violates the phase's purpose.\n- **Confusing analysis with design.** OOA models the problem domain (e.g., a college), not the software solution.\n- **Omitting relationships.** The college model is incomplete without the links between `Student`, `Teacher`, `Course`, and `Department`.\n- **Treating objects as mere data.** Objects in OOA carry responsibilities, not just attributes.\n- **Inventing entities not in the domain.** Only objects that exist in the problem domain should be modelled.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Define OOA precisely: it examines the problem domain in terms of **objects, classes, responsibilities, and relationships**.\n- Memorize the college example and be ready to reproduce the diagram showing `Student`, `Teacher`, and `Course` connected to `Department`.\n- Emphasize the phrase **\"before implementation details are finalized\"** — this is the distinguishing feature of the analysis phase.\n- Use the comparative table to answer \"difference between analysis and implementation\" questions.\n- When asked for an example, use the College System: `Student`, `Teacher`, `Course`, `Department`.\n- Remember the four analysis elements in order: **objects → classes → responsibilities → relationships**.\n\n---",
