          "notes": "## OO Design\n\nOO design converts the results of object-oriented analysis into an implementable structure.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nClass: Student\n────────────────────\nAttributes:\n- studentID\n- name\n- course\n\nMethods:\n+ register()\n+ attendClass()\n+ viewResult()\n```\n\nThe design defines how classes interact and how their responsibilities are distributed.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Object-Oriented (OO) Design** is the phase of the software development lifecycle that transforms the conceptual models produced during **object-oriented analysis (OOA)** into a concrete, implementable blueprint. Where analysis answers *what* the system must do, design answers *how* the system will be structured to do it — specifying classes, their attributes and methods, the relationships among classes, and the distribution of responsibilities.\n\nOO design is grounded in the core principles of the object-oriented paradigm: **encapsulation**, **abstraction**, **inheritance**, and **polymorphism**. The output of this phase is a set of design artifacts (class diagrams, interaction diagrams, responsibility assignments) that can be translated directly into source code in an object-oriented language.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe working principle of OO design proceeds through a sequence of refinement steps:\n\n1. **Identify classes from the analysis model.** Each real-world entity or concept discovered during OOA becomes a candidate class (e.g., `Student`).\n2. **Define attributes.** The data each class must hold is declared as attributes (e.g., `studentID`, `name`, `course`).\n3. **Define methods.** The behaviours each class must expose are declared as methods (e.g., `register()`, `attendClass()`, `viewResult()`).\n4. **Assign responsibilities.** Each method is mapped to the class best suited to perform it, following the principle that a class should own the data it operates on.\n5. **Establish interactions.** The design specifies how classes collaborate — which class calls which method on which other class — so that overall system behaviour emerges from these interactions.\n6. **Refine and iterate.** The structure is reviewed for cohesion, coupling, and clarity, and adjusted until it is implementable.\n\nThe central idea is that **responsibilities are distributed across classes**, and the design captures both the *static structure* (classes and relationships) and the *dynamic behaviour* (interactions).\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: The `Student` Class\n\nFrom the given notes, the `Student` class is expressed as:\n\n```text\nClass: Student\n────────────────────\nAttributes:\n- studentID\n- name\n- course\n\nMethods:\n+ register()\n+ attendClass()\n+ viewResult()\n```\n\n- **Attributes** (`studentID`, `name`, `course`) represent the state of a student object.\n- **Methods** (`register()`, `attendClass()`, `viewResult()`) represent the behaviour a student object can perform.\n\n### Example 2: Interaction Between Classes\n\nOO design also defines how classes interact. For instance, a `Student` object may interact with a `Course` object when invoking `register()`:\n\n```text\nStudent ──register()──▶ Course\n```\n\nHere the `Student` class delegates part of its registration responsibility to the `Course` class, illustrating **responsibility distribution** across collaborating classes.\n\n### Example 3: Responsibility Distribution\n\n| Class     | Responsibility                          |\n|-----------|-----------------------------------------|\n| `Student` | Hold student data; register, attend, view results |\n| `Course`  | Hold course data; manage enrolment       |\n\nThis shows that design is not only about individual classes but about **how responsibilities are divided** among them.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nOO design can be described formally in terms of sets and relations.\n\nLet the set of classes be:\n\n$$\nC = \\{c_1, c_2, \\dots, c_n\\}\n$$\n\nEach class $c_i$ is defined as a tuple of attributes and methods:\n\n$$\nc_i = (A_i, M_i)\n$$\n\nwhere $A_i$ is the set of attributes and $M_i$ is the set of methods of class $c_i$.\n\nFor the `Student` class:\n\n$$\nA_{Student} = \\{\\text{studentID}, \\text{name}, \\text{course}\\}\n$$\n\n$$\nM_{Student} = \\{\\text{register}(), \\text{attendClass}(), \\text{viewResult}()\\}\n$$\n\nInteractions between classes can be modelled as a relation:\n\n$$\nR \\subseteq C \\times C\n$$\n\nwhere $(c_i, c_j) \\in R$ means class $c_i$ interacts with class $c_j$. For example:\n\n$$\n(\\text{Student}, \\text{Course}) \\in R\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect              | OO Analysis (OOA)                     | OO Design (OOD)                          |\n|---------------------|---------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| **Focus**           | What the system must do               | How the system will be structured        |\n| **Output**          | Conceptual model of real-world entities | Implementable class structure          |\n| **Classes**         | Identified from the problem domain    | Refined with attributes and methods      |\n| **Responsibilities**| Recognised at a high level            | Explicitly distributed across classes    |\n| **Interactions**    | Not fully specified                   | Defined between classes                  |\n| **Goal**            | Understanding the problem             | Producing a blueprint for implementation |\n\nThe given notes state that OO design **converts the results of OOA into an implementable structure** — this table highlights that transformation.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Confusing analysis with design.** Treating the conceptual model from OOA as if it were already implementable, without refining attributes, methods, and interactions.\n2. **Poor responsibility distribution.** Assigning methods to classes that do not own the relevant data, leading to weak cohesion.\n3. **Ignoring interactions.** Designing classes in isolation without defining how they interact, even though the notes stress that design defines *how classes interact*.\n4. **Overloading a single class.** Placing too many attributes and methods in one class instead of distributing responsibilities.\n5. **Skipping the implementable structure.** Producing a design that cannot be directly translated into code.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Define OO design precisely:** emphasise that it *converts OOA results into an implementable structure*.\n- **Use the `Student` example** to illustrate attributes, methods, and the class notation:\n\n  ```text\n  Class: Student\n  ────────────────────\n  Attributes:\n  - studentID\n  - name\n  - course\n\n  Methods:\n  + register()\n  + attendClass()\n  + viewResult()\n  ```\n\n- **Highlight two key ideas** from the notes: (1) design defines *how classes interact*, and (2) design defines *how responsibilities are distributed*.\n- **Contrast OOA and OOD** using the comparative table to show the transformation from analysis to design.\n- **Remember the notation:** `-` denotes attributes, `+` denotes methods.\n- **Link to OO principles** (encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism) when explaining why the design is implementable.",
