          "summary": "Complete study notes on Test Case: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Test Case\n\nA test case is a documented set of inputs, conditions, execution steps, and expected results used to verify a specific software behavior.\n\nTest Case Structure:\n\n```text\n┌────────────────────────────┐\n│ Test Case ID               │\n├────────────────────────────┤\n│ Objective                  │\n├────────────────────────────┤\n│ Preconditions              │\n├────────────────────────────┤\n│ Input Data                 │\n├────────────────────────────┤\n│ Execution Steps            │\n├────────────────────────────┤\n│ Expected Result            │\n├────────────────────────────┤\n│ Actual Result              │\n├────────────────────────────┤\n│ Status: Pass / Fail        │\n└────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nTest Case ID: LOGIN-001\nInput: Valid username + valid password\nExpected: User reaches dashboard\nActual: User reaches dashboard\nStatus: PASS\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **test case** is a formalized artifact in software verification and validation that encapsulates a documented set of **inputs**, **conditions**, **execution steps**, and **expected results** used to verify a specific software behavior. It serves as the atomic unit of test execution — the smallest independently executable and verifiable item that a tester runs against the system under test.\n\nEach test case is designed to exercise one identifiable behavior or requirement, producing an observable outcome that can be compared against the **expected result** to yield a binary verdict of **Pass** or **Fail**. The test case thus bridges the gap between abstract requirements and concrete, repeatable verification activity.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe working principle of a test case rests on **controlled, repeatable verification**:\n\n1. **Preconditions** are established so the system is in a known, valid starting state.\n2. **Input Data** is supplied exactly as documented, ensuring determinism.\n3. **Execution Steps** are followed in the prescribed order, removing ambiguity in how the behavior is triggered.\n4. The system produces an **Actual Result**, which is captured and recorded.\n5. The **Actual Result** is compared against the **Expected Result**.\n6. A **Status** of **Pass** or **Fail** is assigned based on the comparison.\n\nThis structure ensures that any tester, at any time, can reproduce the same verification and reach the same verdict, which is the foundation of trustworthy testing.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Positive Login (from existing notes)\n\n```text\nTest Case ID: LOGIN-001\nInput: Valid username + valid password\nExpected: User reaches dashboard\nActual: User reaches dashboard\nStatus: PASS\n```\n\nHere the **Objective** is to verify that valid credentials grant access. The **Precondition** is that a registered account exists. The **Expected Result** is reaching the dashboard, and the observed **Actual Result** matches, yielding **PASS**.\n\n### Example 2 — Negative Login\n\n```text\nTest Case ID: LOGIN-002\nInput: Valid username + invalid password\nExpected: Access denied / error message\nActual: Access denied / error message\nStatus: PASS\n```\n\nThis test case verifies the complementary behavior: that invalid credentials are rejected. Note that the **Status** is **PASS** because the system behaved as expected — a test case passes when actual behavior matches expected behavior, regardless of whether the scenario is \"positive\" or \"negative.\"\n\n### Example 3 — Failure Detection\n\n```text\nTest Case ID: LOGIN-003\nInput: Valid username + valid password\nExpected: User reaches dashboard\nActual: User remains on login page\nStatus: FAIL\n```\n\nHere the **Actual Result** diverges from the **Expected Result**, so the **Status** is **FAIL**, indicating a defect in the login behavior.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nA test case can be modeled as a verification function. Let the system under test be a function \\( S \\) that maps an input \\( x \\) and precondition state \\( p \\) to an observed output:\n\n\\[\nS(p, x) = y_{actual}\n\\]\n\nThe test case defines an expected output \\( y_{expected} \\). The verdict is then:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Status} =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{PASS}, & \\text{if } y_{actual} = y_{expected} \\\\\n\\text{FAIL}, & \\text{if } y_{actual} \\neq y_{expected}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFor a suite of \\( n \\) test cases, the pass rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Pass Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Number of PASS test cases}}{n} \\times 100\\%\n\\]\n\nEach test case contributes exactly one verdict, making the suite a collection of independent binary outcomes.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Attribute | Test Case | Related Artifact |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Granularity** | Single behavior | Test suite groups many test cases |\n| **Content** | ID, Objective, Preconditions, Input, Steps, Expected, Actual, Status | Varies by artifact |\n| **Verdict** | Pass / Fail | Suite aggregates verdicts |\n| **Reusability** | Repeatable with same inputs | Depends on scope |\n| **Purpose** | Verify a specific software behavior | Broader verification goals |\n\nThe distinguishing feature of a test case is its **complete, self-contained structure** — it carries everything needed to execute and judge a single behavior.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Omitting Preconditions** — without a documented starting state, results become non-reproducible.\n- **Vague Expected Results** — an expected result must be observable and unambiguous so the Pass/Fail comparison is objective.\n- **Skipping Execution Steps** — undocumented steps invite inconsistent execution across testers.\n- **Confusing FAIL with a \"negative\" scenario** — a negative test case still yields **PASS** when the system behaves as expected.\n- **Leaving Actual Result blank** — the Actual Result is required to justify the Status.\n- **Not recording Status** — without an explicit Pass/Fail, the test case provides no verdict.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the **eight structural fields**: Test Case ID, Objective, Preconditions, Input Data, Execution Steps, Expected Result, Actual Result, Status.\n- Remember the definition verbatim: *inputs, conditions, execution steps, and expected results used to verify a specific software behavior*.\n- For scenario questions, always compare **Expected Result** vs **Actual Result** to determine **Status**.\n- A **PASS** means actual matches expected — even for negative scenarios like invalid credentials.\n- A **FAIL** means actual diverges from expected, signaling a defect.\n- Use the **Test Case ID** convention (e.g., `LOGIN-001`) to show you understand traceability and identification.\n- When asked to write a test case, reproduce the full structure — partial answers lose marks.",
