          "id": "test-criteria",
          "title": "Test Criteria",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 3 Concept 16",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Test Criteria: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Test Criteria\n\nTest criteria are rules used to determine whether testing is sufficient or whether a particular test has achieved its objective.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Requirement coverage\n- Statement coverage\n- Branch coverage\n- Path coverage\n- Condition coverage\n- Functional coverage\n\nCoverage Concept:\n\n```text\nPROGRAM\n                │\n       ┌────────┼────────┐\n       ▼        ▼        ▼\n   Statements Branches  Paths\n       │        │        │\n       └────────┼────────┘\n                ▼\n          Test Coverage\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Test criteria** (also referred to as *test adequacy criteria* or *coverage criteria*) are formal rules that define the conditions under which a software testing process is considered **sufficient** or when an individual test is deemed to have **achieved its objective**. They provide an objective, measurable basis for deciding when to stop testing and for evaluating the thoroughness of a test suite.\n\nIn academic software engineering, a test criterion acts as a **stopping rule** and a **measurement instrument**: it specifies *what* must be exercised in the program (or requirements) and *how much* of it must be exercised before testing is declared complete. The criteria listed above — requirement, statement, branch, path, condition, and functional coverage — represent different *granularities* and *targets* of coverage, ranging from high-level requirements down to low-level control-flow constructs.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe underlying principle of test criteria is **coverage measurement**: a program (or its specification) is decomposed into *coverage items* (also called *test obligations* or *targets*). A test suite is then executed, and the criterion measures the proportion of coverage items that have been exercised.\n\nThe general working process is:\n\n1. **Identify the coverage domain** — e.g., statements, branches, paths, conditions, or requirements.\n2. **Enumerate coverage items** — every statement, every branch outcome, every feasible path, etc.\n3. **Execute the test suite** — run tests and record which items are exercised.\n4. **Compute coverage** — the ratio of exercised items to total items.\n5. **Apply the stopping rule** — testing is sufficient when coverage reaches the criterion's threshold (typically 100% for the structural criteria).\n\nThe diagram in the notes illustrates this decomposition: a **PROGRAM** is broken down into **Statements**, **Branches**, and **Paths**, and these collectively feed into the notion of **Test Coverage**.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Requirement Coverage\nEach requirement in the specification must be traced to at least one test case. If a requirement has no associated test, coverage is incomplete.\n\n### Statement Coverage\nEvery executable statement in the program must be executed at least once by the test suite.\n\n### Branch Coverage\nEvery branch (decision outcome — true and false) must be taken at least once.\n\n### Path Coverage\nEvery feasible path through the control-flow graph must be executed. This is the strongest (and most expensive) structural criterion.\n\n### Condition Coverage\nEvery atomic boolean condition within a decision must evaluate to both true and false at least once.\n\n### Functional Coverage\nCoverage is measured against functional features or behaviors defined in the specification (e.g., feature checklists), rather than against code structure.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nCoverage is generally expressed as a ratio:\n\n$$\n\\text{Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Number of coverage items exercised}}{\\text{Total number of coverage items}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nFor the structural criteria, this specializes as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Statement Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Statements executed}}{\\text{Total statements}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Branch Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Branches taken}}{\\text{Total branches}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Path Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Paths executed}}{\\text{Total feasible paths}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Condition Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Conditions evaluated both ways}}{\\text{Total conditions}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nA test suite is *adequate* with respect to a criterion when the corresponding coverage equals the criterion's required threshold (commonly 100%).\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Criterion | Coverage Target | Granularity | Relative Strength | Typical Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Requirement coverage | Requirements | High-level | Specification-oriented | Low–Medium |\n| Statement coverage | Statements | Low-level | Weakest structural | Low |\n| Branch coverage | Branches (decision outcomes) | Low-level | Stronger than statement | Medium |\n| Path coverage | Paths | Low-level | Strongest structural | High |\n| Condition coverage | Atomic conditions | Low-level | Focuses on boolean sub-expressions | Medium–High |\n| Functional coverage | Functional features | Specification/feature level | Behavior-oriented | Medium |\n\n**Key relationships:**\n- Path coverage **subsumes** branch coverage, which in turn **subsumes** statement coverage.\n- Requirement and functional coverage are **specification-based**, whereas statement, branch, path, and condition coverage are **structure-based**.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing coverage with correctness.** High coverage does not guarantee the absence of defects; it only indicates that items were exercised.\n- **Assuming 100% statement coverage implies 100% branch coverage.** It does not — branch coverage is strictly stronger.\n- **Ignoring infeasible paths.** Path coverage counts only *feasible* paths; infeasible paths cannot be executed.\n- **Treating condition coverage as equivalent to branch coverage.** Condition coverage targets atomic conditions, not whole decisions.\n- **Skipping requirement/functional coverage** in favor of only structural metrics, leaving specification-level gaps untested.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the **list of test criteria**: requirement, statement, branch, path, condition, and functional coverage.\n- Remember the **subsumption hierarchy**: Path ⊇ Branch ⊇ Statement.\n- Be ready to **compute coverage** using the ratio formula.\n- Distinguish **specification-based** criteria (requirement, functional) from **structure-based** criteria (statement, branch, path, condition).\n- Use the **PROGRAM → Statements/Branches/Paths → Test Coverage** diagram to explain how coverage is derived.\n- A common exam question: *\"Define test criteria and list examples.\"* — answer with the definition and the six examples above.",
