          "title": "Structural Testing",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 3 Concept 18",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Structural Testing: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Structural Testing\n\nStructural testing examines the internal structure and logic of the program.\n\nIt is also called white-box testing.\n\nIt may examine:\n\n- Statements\n- Branches\n- Conditions\n- Paths\n- Loops\n- Control flow\n\nStructural Testing:\n\n```text\nSOURCE CODE\n                   │\n                   ▼\n            CONTROL FLOW\n                   │\n        ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n        ▼          ▼          ▼\n    Statements  Branches     Paths\n        │          │          │\n        └──────────┼──────────┘\n                   ▼\n               TEST CASES\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Structural Testing** is a software testing technique in which test cases are derived from the **internal structure and logic of the program** rather than from its external specification. Because the tester must have knowledge of the source code, it is classified as **white-box testing** (also called glass-box or clear-box testing).\n\nFormally, structural testing defines test adequacy criteria based on the **control flow** of the program. The program is modeled as a graph (control flow graph), and coverage criteria are defined over elements of that graph — statements, branches, conditions, paths, and loops.\n\n> **Key idea:** The tester looks *inside* the code and designs inputs that exercise specific internal elements.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. **Source code is analyzed** to build a control flow representation.\n2. **Control flow elements** (statements, branches, conditions, paths, loops) are identified.\n3. **Coverage criteria** are selected (e.g., statement coverage, branch coverage).\n4. **Test cases are designed** so that the chosen elements are executed.\n5. **Coverage is measured** to determine how much of the structure has been exercised.\n\nThe flow from source code to test cases is illustrated below:\n\n```text\nSOURCE CODE\n                   │\n                   ▼\n            CONTROL FLOW\n                   │\n        ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n        ▼          ▼          ▼\n    Statements  Branches     Paths\n        │          │          │\n        └──────────┼──────────┘\n                   ▼\n               TEST CASES\n```\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Statement Coverage\n\n```text\nif (x > 0)\n    y = x;      // Statement S1\nelse\n    y = -x;     // Statement S2\n```\n\n- Test with `x = 5` → executes S1.\n- Test with `x = -5` → executes S2.\n- Both tests together achieve **100% statement coverage**.\n\n### Example 2 — Branch Coverage\n\nUsing the same code:\n\n- `x = 5` covers the **true branch** of the `if`.\n- `x = -5` covers the **false branch** (the `else`).\n- Branch coverage requires both the true and false outcomes of every decision.\n\n### Example 3 — Path Coverage\n\nFor a program with two sequential decisions, there are up to **four paths**:\n\n```text\nDecision A ──► Decision B ──► Path\n   T              T            P1\n   T              F            P2\n   F              T            P3\n   F              F            P4\n```\n\nPath coverage requires test cases that traverse each of these paths.\n\n### Example 4 — Loop Testing\n\nLoops are examined for:\n\n- Zero iterations (loop body skipped)\n- One iteration\n- Typical number of iterations\n- Maximum iterations\n- Boundary conditions at loop entry and exit\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nCoverage is typically expressed as a ratio:\n\n$$\n\\text{Statement Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Statements Executed}}{\\text{Total Statements}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Branch Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Branches Executed}}{\\text{Total Branches}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Path Coverage} = \\frac{\\text{Paths Executed}}{\\text{Total Paths}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nFor a program with $d$ independent binary decisions, the number of possible paths is bounded by:\n\n$$\n\\text{Paths} \\leq 2^{d}\n$$\n\nThis exponential growth is why **exhaustive path coverage** is often impractical for large programs.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Criterion | What It Exercises | Strength | Limitation |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------|------------|\n| **Statement Coverage** | Every statement | Weakest structural criterion | May miss untested branches |\n| **Branch Coverage** | Every branch outcome | Stronger than statement | Does not test all condition combinations |\n| **Condition Coverage** | Each condition outcome | Tests individual conditions | May not test branch combinations |\n| **Path Coverage** | Every path | Strongest | Exponential number of paths |\n| **Loop Testing** | Loop entry/exit/boundaries | Targets loop-specific faults | Requires boundary analysis |\n\n**Structural vs. Functional Testing:**\n\n| Aspect | Structural (White-Box) | Functional (Black-Box) |\n|--------|------------------------|------------------------|\n| Basis | Internal structure/logic | External specification |\n| Knowledge required | Source code | Requirements |\n| Elements examined | Statements, branches, conditions, paths, loops, control flow | Inputs and outputs |\n| Typical goal | Coverage of code elements | Validation of behavior |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing statement and branch coverage** — executing a statement does not guarantee both outcomes of a decision are tested.\n- **Assuming 100% statement coverage is sufficient** — it can leave branches and conditions untested.\n- **Ignoring loop boundaries** — zero, one, and maximum iterations are frequently overlooked.\n- **Attempting exhaustive path coverage** on large programs — the number of paths grows exponentially.\n- **Forgetting that structural testing requires source code access** — it cannot be applied purely from a specification.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the **synonym**: Structural Testing = **White-Box Testing**.\n- Memorize the **six elements** it may examine: **statements, branches, conditions, paths, loops, control flow**.\n- Be ready to **draw the control flow diagram** from source code to test cases (as shown above).\n- Use the **coverage formulas** to compute percentages in numerical questions.\n- Recall the **path bound** $\\text{Paths} \\leq 2^{d}$ for $d$ binary decisions.\n- In comparison questions, contrast **structural (white-box)** with **functional (black-box)** testing.\n- Link each coverage criterion to its **strength and limitation** — this is a common short-answer question.",
