          "id": "integration-testing",
          "title": "Integration Testing",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 3 Concept 25",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Integration Testing: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Integration Testing\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Integration Testing** is a level of software testing where individually tested modules (unit-tested components) are combined and verified as a group. Its central purpose is to expose defects in the **interfaces** and **interactions** between integrated units. Unlike unit testing, which validates a module in isolation, integration testing validates the **communication, data transfer, and control flow** across module boundaries.\n\nIntegration testing occupies the middle layer of the classical testing hierarchy:\n\n```text\nUnit Testing  ──►  Integration Testing  ──►  System Testing\n(modules alone)    (modules combined)        (whole system)\n```\n\nThe fundamental object under test is not a single module but the **integrated system** formed by composing modules:\n\n```text\nModule A ───┐\n             │\n Module B ───┼──► Integrated System\n             │\n Module C ───┘\n```\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe working principle of integration testing is **incremental composition and verification**. Modules that have already passed unit testing are combined step by step, and after each combination the interfaces between them are exercised. The process proceeds as follows:\n\n```text\nA Test\n  │\n  ▼\nA + B\n  │\n  ▼\nA + B + C\n  │\n  ▼\nA + B + C + D\n  │\n  ▼\nIntegrated System\n```\n\nAt each stage, the tester checks that:\n\n- **Interfaces** between modules are correctly defined and invoked.\n- **Data transfer** across module boundaries is correct (parameters, return values, shared data).\n- **Communication** between modules occurs as intended.\n- **Module interactions** produce the expected combined behavior.\n- **Integration-related defects** (mismatched assumptions, interface errors) are detected.\n\nBecause each increment is verified before the next module is added, defects can be localized to the most recently integrated boundary rather than being buried in the fully assembled system.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Example 1 — Incremental build-up (A → A+B → A+B+C → A+B+C+D).**\nFollowing the integration process shown above, module A is tested first. Then B is added and the A–B interface is verified. Then C is added and the A+B–C boundary is verified, and so on, until the integrated system is reached. Each arrow in the diagram represents a verification step at a newly created interface.\n\n**Example 2 — Interface-focused verification.**\nConsider the integrated system composed of Module A, Module B, and Module C. Integration testing focuses on the junctions between them — the points where A calls B, where B calls C, and where data flows between them — rather than re-testing the internal logic of each module (which unit testing already covered).\n\n**Example 3 — Integration-related defects.**\nA defect that only appears when modules are combined (for example, a mismatch in how one module supplies data and another expects it) is an integration-related defect. Such defects are invisible during unit testing of the individual modules and are the primary target of integration testing.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nIntegration testing can be modeled in terms of the number of interfaces exercised as modules are combined. If an integrated system is built from modules $M_1, M_2, \\dots, M_n$, the incremental process verifies one new combination at each step:\n\n$$\n\\text{Steps} = n - 1\n$$\n\nwhere each step corresponds to one arrow in the integration process:\n\n$$\nM_1 \\rightarrow M_1{+}M_2 \\rightarrow M_1{+}M_2{+}M_3 \\rightarrow \\dots \\rightarrow \\text{Integrated System}\n$$\n\nThe set of interfaces under test at any stage is the set of boundaries between the currently combined modules. For a combination of $k$ modules, the interfaces to verify are the connections among those $k$ modules:\n\n$$\n\\text{Interfaces}(k) = \\text{connections among } M_1, \\dots, M_k\n$$\n\nThe focus of verification at each stage is the tuple:\n\n$$\n(\\text{Interface},\\ \\text{Data Transfer},\\ \\text{Communication},\\ \\text{Module Interaction})\n$$\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Unit Testing | Integration Testing | System Testing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Object under test | Individual module | Combined modules / integrated system | Whole system |\n| Primary focus | Module logic in isolation | Interfaces, data transfer, communication, module interactions | End-to-end system behavior |\n| Defects targeted | Internal module defects | Integration-related defects | System-level defects |\n| Build-up | Module tested alone | A → A+B → A+B+C → A+B+C+D → Integrated System | Fully integrated system |\n| Key concern | Correctness of one unit | Correctness of boundaries between units | Correctness of the complete product |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing integration testing with unit testing.** Unit testing validates modules individually; integration testing validates the **interactions** between them. Re-testing module internals during integration misses the point.\n- **Ignoring interfaces.** The core targets — interfaces, data transfer, communication, and module interactions — are often skipped in favor of re-running unit-level checks.\n- **Skipping incremental steps.** Jumping straight to the integrated system instead of following the A → A+B → A+B+C → A+B+C+D progression makes it harder to localize integration-related defects.\n- **Overlooking integration-related defects.** Defects that arise only from combining modules are the specific responsibility of integration testing and will not surface in unit testing.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Define integration testing precisely: it **combines individually tested modules and verifies their interactions**.\n- Memorize the focus list: **Interfaces, Data transfer, Communication, Module interactions, Integration-related defects**.\n- Be ready to reproduce the integration process diagram: **A Test → A + B → A + B + C → A + B + C + D → Integrated System**.\n- Remember the position in the testing hierarchy: after unit testing, before system testing.\n- Use the comparative table to answer \"difference between unit and integration testing\" questions.\n- Emphasize that the target of integration testing is the **integrated system**, not individual modules.",
