          "id": "software-configuration-item",
          "title": "Software Configuration Item",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 4 Concept 26",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Software Configuration Item: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Software Configuration Item\n\nA Software Configuration Item (SCI) is a software artifact placed under configuration management.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nSOFTWARE PROJECT\n                       │\n        ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐\n        ▼              ▼              ▼\n   Requirements      Source Code    Test Cases\n        │              │              ▼\n        ▼              ▼         Build Files\n      Design        Build Files    Documents\n```\n\nEach item can have an identified version and controlled change history.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **Software Configuration Item (SCI)** is any identifiable software artifact — including documents, source code, test cases, build files, and design specifications — that is formally designated for placement under **configuration management**. Once an artifact is designated as an SCI, it becomes a controlled entity: it receives a unique identifier, a version designation, and a recorded change history. The SCI is the fundamental unit of control in software configuration management (SCM); configuration management activities such as version control, change control, and status accounting operate on SCIs as their primary objects.\n\nAn SCI is not merely a file — it is a **managed entity**. The act of declaring an artifact an SCI is itself a management decision, because it determines which artifacts are subject to formal change control and which are not.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe SCI concept operates on the following principle:\n\n1. **Identification** — A software artifact (requirement, design, source code, test case, build file, or document) is selected for configuration management.\n2. **Designation** — The artifact is formally declared an SCI, giving it a unique identity within the project.\n3. **Versioning** — Each SCI can have an identified version, so that distinct states of the artifact are distinguishable.\n4. **Change History** — Each SCI maintains a controlled change history, recording how and when the artifact evolved.\n5. **Control** — Because the SCI has an identity, a version, and a history, changes to it can be controlled, traced, and audited.\n\nIn short: **identification → versioning → controlled change history** is the lifecycle that turns an ordinary artifact into an SCI.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\nThe diagram above groups SCIs by category within a software project:\n\n| Category | Example SCIs |\n|---|---|\n| Requirements | Requirements documents |\n| Design | Design documents |\n| Source Code | Source code files |\n| Test Cases | Test cases |\n| Build Files | Build files |\n| Documents | Project documents |\n\nEach of these artifacts, once placed under configuration management, becomes an SCI with its own identified version and controlled change history. For example:\n\n- A **requirements document** becomes an SCI when it is baselined and versioned.\n- A **source code file** becomes an SCI when it is checked into configuration management with a tracked version.\n- A **test case** becomes an SCI when it is placed under the same controlled change history as the code it validates.\n- A **build file** becomes an SCI when its versions are tracked alongside the source it builds.\n- A **design document** becomes an SCI when its revisions are controlled.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe notes describe three properties of an SCI: identity, version, and change history. These can be expressed abstractly.\n\nLet an SCI be denoted $c$. Each SCI has:\n\n- an **identifier**: $\\text{id}(c)$\n- a **version**: $v(c)$\n- a **change history**: $H(c)$\n\nThe change history can be modeled as an ordered sequence of versions:\n\n$$\nH(c) = \\langle v_0(c),\\; v_1(c),\\; \\dots,\\; v_n(c) \\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $v_0(c)$ is the initial version and $v_n(c)$ is the current version. Each transition represents a controlled change:\n\n$$\nv_i(c) \\;\\rightarrow\\; v_{i+1}(c)\n$$\n\nThe set of all SCIs in a project is:\n\n$$\n\\text{SCI}_{\\text{project}} = \\{\\, c \\mid c \\text{ is placed under configuration management} \\,\\}\n$$\n\nThese formulations use only the properties stated in the notes: identified version and controlled change history.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | SCI (under configuration management) | Ordinary artifact (not under configuration management) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Identity | Has an identified version | No identified version |\n| Change tracking | Controlled change history | No controlled change history |\n| Management | Placed under configuration management | Not placed under configuration management |\n| Examples | Requirements, source code, test cases, design, build files, documents | Any artifact not designated as an SCI |\n\nThe distinguishing factor is not the *type* of artifact — the same kind of artifact (e.g., a document) can be an SCI or not — but whether it has been **placed under configuration management**.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming only source code can be an SCI.** The notes explicitly list requirements, design, source code, test cases, build files, and documents as examples. Any software artifact can be an SCI.\n- **Confusing an artifact with an SCI.** An artifact becomes an SCI only when it is placed under configuration management. The artifact type alone does not make it an SCI.\n- **Ignoring version identity.** Each SCI *can have an identified version* — treating an SCI as a single unversioned entity loses a core property.\n- **Neglecting change history.** A controlled change history is a defining property of an SCI; an artifact without one is not functioning as an SCI.\n- **Treating the SCI list as fixed.** The diagram shows categories, not an exhaustive or closed list.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Define precisely:** An SCI is a software artifact *placed under configuration management* — the phrase \"placed under configuration management\" is the key qualifier.\n- **Memorize the examples from the diagram:** Requirements, Source Code, Test Cases, Design, Build Files, Documents.\n- **Remember the two stated properties:** (1) identified version, (2) controlled change history.\n- **Use the diagram:** Reproduce the SOFTWARE PROJECT tree showing the categories of SCIs.\n- **Contrast with non-SCIs:** Be ready to explain that the same artifact type can be an SCI or not, depending on whether it is under configuration management.\n- **Link to SCM:** The SCI is the unit on which configuration management operates — versioning and change control apply to SCIs.",
