          "id": "validation-techniques",
          "title": "Validation Techniques",
          "subtitle": "CA455 Unit 4 Concept 38",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Validation Techniques: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Validation Techniques\n\nValidation primarily involves executing the software and evaluating whether it satisfies intended requirements.\n\n```text\nSOFTWARE\n                 │\n                 ▼\n            Execute\n                 │\n                 ▼\n          Observe Results\n                 │\n                 ▼\n       Compare with Requirements\n                 │\n            ┌────┴────┐\n            ▼         ▼\n         Satisfies   Does Not\n            │         │\n            ▼         ▼\n          Valid     Defect\n```\n\nTesting is a major validation activity.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Validation** is the process of **executing the software** and **evaluating whether it satisfies intended requirements**. It is fundamentally concerned with answering the question: *\"Are we building the right product?\"*\n\nValidation operates on the **executed behaviour** of the software. The software is run, results are observed, and those results are compared against the intended requirements. Based on this comparison, the software is judged either to **satisfy** the requirements (and is therefore **valid**) or to **not satisfy** them (revealing a **defect**).\n\n**Testing** is identified as a **major validation activity**, meaning that the execution-and-comparison loop described above is realised in practice primarily through testing.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe working principle of validation follows a strict sequential flow, as captured in the original diagram:\n\n1. **Software** — the artefact under evaluation.\n2. **Execute** — the software is run.\n3. **Observe Results** — the outcomes of execution are captured.\n4. **Compare with Requirements** — observed results are checked against intended requirements.\n5. **Decision Point** — two possible outcomes:\n   - **Satisfies** → the software is **Valid**.\n   - **Does Not Satisfy** → a **Defect** is identified.\n\nThis principle emphasises that validation is **execution-driven**: without execution, there is no observation, and without observation, no comparison against requirements can occur.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Satisfies Requirements (Valid)\n\n```text\nSoftware: Login module\nExecute: Enter valid credentials\nObserve Results: User is granted access\nCompare with Requirements: Requirement states valid users must be granted access\nOutcome: Satisfies → Valid\n```\n\n### Example 2 — Does Not Satisfy Requirements (Defect)\n\n```text\nSoftware: Login module\nExecute: Enter valid credentials\nObserve Results: Access denied\nCompare with Requirements: Requirement states valid users must be granted access\nOutcome: Does Not Satisfy → Defect\n```\n\n### Example 3 — Testing as Validation Activity\n\n```text\nSoftware: Any module\nActivity: Testing (major validation activity)\nExecute: Run test cases\nObserve Results: Record actual outcomes\nCompare with Requirements: Match against intended requirements\nOutcome: Satisfies → Valid | Does Not Satisfy → Defect\n```\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet:\n\n- \\( S \\) = the software under validation\n- \\( R \\) = the set of intended requirements\n- \\( E(S) \\) = the observed results of executing \\( S \\)\n\nValidation can be expressed as a comparison function:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Validate}(S, R) =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{Valid}, & \\text{if } E(S) \\text{ satisfies } R \\\\\n\\text{Defect}, & \\text{if } E(S) \\text{ does not satisfy } R\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, defining a satisfaction predicate \\( \\text{Sat}(E(S), R) \\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Outcome} =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{Valid}, & \\text{Sat}(E(S), R) = \\text{true} \\\\\n\\text{Defect}, & \\text{Sat}(E(S), R) = \\text{false}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe decision point in the diagram corresponds exactly to the branching of this predicate.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Satisfies | Does Not Satisfy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Diagram branch | Left branch | Right branch |\n| Result | Valid | Defect |\n| Meaning | Software meets intended requirements | Software fails intended requirements |\n| Action implied | Accept as valid | Identify as defect |\n\n| Aspect | Validation (as described) |\n|---|---|\n| Core action | Executing the software |\n| Basis of judgement | Observed results vs. intended requirements |\n| Primary activity | Testing |\n| Output | Valid or Defect |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Skipping execution.** Validation *primarily involves executing the software*. Without execution, there are no results to observe and nothing to compare.\n- **Skipping observation.** Executing without observing results breaks the flow between *Execute* and *Compare with Requirements*.\n- **Skipping comparison.** Observing results without comparing them to intended requirements cannot yield a Valid/Defect decision.\n- **Confusing the branches.** *Satisfies* leads to **Valid**; *Does Not* leads to **Defect**. Reversing these misrepresents the outcome.\n- **Overlooking testing.** Testing is a **major validation activity** and must not be treated as separate from validation.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorise the exact flow: **Software → Execute → Observe Results → Compare with Requirements → Satisfies / Does Not → Valid / Defect**.\n- Be ready to reproduce the diagram precisely, including both branches at the decision point.\n- Remember the key phrase: validation *primarily involves executing the software and evaluating whether it satisfies intended requirements*.\n- State clearly that **testing is a major validation activity**.\n- Use the terms **Valid** and **Defect** exactly as given — they are the two terminal outcomes of the validation flow.\n- When explaining, always anchor your answer to the execution-and-comparison principle rather than to any non-execution activity.",
